                question: "CAP Definition: \n1 - Action plans defined using a combination of safety controls layered to include all levels of the hierarchy of control (Elimination, Substitution, Engineering, Work Practice/Administrative, PPE)\n2- Mitigation Action: Effective mitigating actions are in-place to reduce risk for the remaining gaps where the systemic long-term actions defined in the CAP have not yet been completed.",
                intent: "Intent:\nA corrective action plan is a structured approach used to fix a risk/ problem that has already been identified and to prevent it from happening again. It focuses on addressing the root cause, making long‑term changes, and ensuring the issue is fully resolved. If the site's analysis documents that no similar gaps exist at the facility, or that any gaps are addressed through a prior CAP (e.g for a prior CTA, LSR, SIF/SIFp, etc.), then no additional CAP is necessary so long as the prior CAP is referenced and the prior CAP really does address the issues.\n\nMitigation actions are actions/ steps taken to reduce or limit the impact of a problem or risk, often as a temporary solution. They help manage the situation and minimize harm until a permanent solution can be implemented. \n\nCAP Definition (0. 4, 7.5, 11, 15):\n15:  Action plans are defined for all applicable Calls to Action, AND, Safety controls are layered to include all levels of the hierarchy of controls (Elimination, Substitution, Engineering, Work Practice/Administrative, PPE) and are applied to all applicable lines, equip, etc., AND, a responsbile person/owner and realistic deadline of each action are clearly defined \n11:  All elements of 10 pt criteria, and some, but not all, elements of 20 point criteria are in place. For example <100% of CTAs have action plans meeting the 20 pt criteria.\n7.5: Action plans are defined but do not cover all required actions, OR Mostly consider only Level 1 (PPE) and Level 2 (Administrative controls); AND/OR  the responsible person/owner and deadline are not defined for all actions OR action plans are only defined for ~50% of CTAs. \n4: Some aspects of 10 or 20 point criteria are implemented, but not all elements of 10 point criteria are in place.\n0: No action was defined, OR limited to communication.\n\n Mitigation Actions: (0, 2.5, 5, 7.5, 10)\n10: Mitigation actions are defined and documented for all actions requiring time for closure, AND, are fully implemented AND effective\n7.5: All elements of 5 pt criteria, and some, but not all, elements of 10 point criteria are in place. For example most mitigation actions are defined and implemented OR only a few mitigation actions are not implemented or not effective\n5: Some / Limited mitigation actions are defined and implemented, OR, mitigation actions are not documented but are implemented (evidence must be provided during the audit), OR, Not Effective\n2.5:  Some aspects of 5 or 10 point criteria are implemented, but not all elements of 5 point criteria are in place. For example, mitigation actions are not documented, or only a few are implemented.\n0: No mitigation actions defined or not implemented",
